{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794547333018,"sessionID":"ses_e2ca66d86ffeQHDVXGxsL6fzIZ","part":{"id":"prt_1d35b379a001pTLKYBEwTfvEsk","sessionID":"ses_e2ca66d86ffeQHDVXGxsL6fzIZ","messageID":"msg_1d35b31f800166Otr65YOMHC3l","type":"step-start","snapshot":"7f1e422b40581c87adcd6496c6a495d4839c8768"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794547333792,"sessionID":"ses_e2ca66d86ffeQHDVXGxsL6fzIZ","part":{"partID":"prt_1d35b391a001OhwbPRFE2QplOe","sessionID":"ses_e2ca66d86ffeQHDVXGxsL6fzIZ","messageID":"msg_1d35b31f800166Otr65YOMHC3l","type":"tool","id":"call_c455cdd274464a408021568f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md, lines 1-203\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-skilled\n3: description: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.1.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8:   packetKind: surface\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n12: \n13: **Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n14: \n15: Detection has two steps:\n16: \n17: - First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n18: - Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n19:   - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n20:   - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n21:   - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n22:   - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n23:   - `.rs` -> Rust\n24:   - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n25: \n26: For Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\n27: napi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n28: \n29: ## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n30: \n31: - The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n32: - The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n33: - This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n38: \n39: The hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n40: \n41: The parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n42: \n43: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n48: \n49: Language standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n50: - TypeScript:\n51:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n52:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n53:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n54:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n55:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n56:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n57:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n58: - Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n59: - Shell:\n60:   - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n61:   - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n62:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n63:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n64:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n65:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n66: - Rust:\n67:   - Style guide:\n68:     - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n69:     - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n70:     - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n71:     - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n72:     - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n73:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n74:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n75:   - Quality standards:\n76:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n77:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n78:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n79:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n80:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n81:   - Quick reference:\n82:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n83:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n84:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n85:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n86:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n87: - Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n88: - JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n89: \n90: Language-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n91: - `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n92: - `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n93: - `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n94: - `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n95: \n96: Authoring and validation assets:\n97: \n98: - Checklists:\n99:   - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n100:   - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n101:   - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n102:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n103:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n104:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n105:   - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n106:   - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n107:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n108:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n109:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n110:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n111:   - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n112:   - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n113:   - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n114:   - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n115: - Scripts:\n116:   - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n117: - References:\n118:   - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n119:   - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n120: \n121: Workflow: `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md` link to the hub's shared implement -> debug -> verify doctrine in `../../shared/references/`; the split shared, language, hook and alignment resources above apply on top of it.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 4. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n126: \n127: - **Plugins never write to the TUI.** OpenCode plugins must not print to the process stdout/stderr (no overlay on the chat input); user/agent-visible output goes through system-context injection, tools, or append-only log files; DEBUG-gated stderr is allowed only behind an env flag. See `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md` and the plugin exemption tier.\n128: - **Descriptors are load-bearing.** `graph-metadata.json` / `description.json` shape drives discovery; validate JSON/JSONC against `references/config/quality-standards.md`.\n129: - **Alignment drift is a verification gate.** Before any completion claim, re-run the four live sk-code drift guards on a system-code change through the single entry point `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh`.\n130:   - `assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` checks language integrity, plus dead RESOURCE_MAP routes with `--check-router`.\n131:   - `assets/scripts/verify_stack_folders.py` checks that language reference folders resolve.\n132:   - `assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs` is the router-sync guard. It restores the four checks of the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite, which was deleted, and the wrapper runs every leg of it: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4.\n133:     - Check (1): router paths exist on disk, every routable doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed.\n134:     - Check (2): the parent RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier.\n135:     - Check (3): compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and this RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`.\n136:     - Check (4): every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router.\n137:   - The dead-path part of check (1) is also checked by `--check-router`. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc walk, fails on any reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink.\n138:   - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4) in CI only, with the admission step warn-only.\n139:   - The documentation claim guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) reads the sk-code prose the other guards skip, from the hub folder on disk.\n140:     It checks that path references resolve and that no retired packet name remains.\n141:     It also checks that every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json`, and that every file the `ROUTER.md` load-tier prose says loads on every route is in `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.\n142:     Lines that keep a legacy name on purpose sit in its allowlist with a reason. See `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`.\n143:   The wrapper scans repository content only: the files git tracks, so ignored trees, sibling worktrees and vendored clones stay out of a gate they can never pass. It exits 0 on a clean tree, so a completion claim requires wrapper rc 0 rather than a delta against a backlog.\n144: - **Rust preserves the TypeScript contract.** Rust napi-rs, WASM/WASI, and sidecar modules are compatibility implementations, not independent behavior authorities. JS-visible bytes, six-decimal numeric behavior, comparator tie-breaks, deterministic IDs, collection order, DTOs, and error shapes must remain identical to the TypeScript oracle.\n145: - **Touched-language set, not one-per-task.** Most `.skilled/` tasks touch a single language. Keep that slice tight and use the shared tier for cross-language rules.\n146: \n147: An interop task that spans a language pair, such as a napi-rs / WASM / sidecar Rust module held to its TypeScript oracle, legitimately touches both languages.\n148: The router selects the languages the task actually touches and loads both trios plus the shared tier.\n149: You cannot hold Rust byte-identical to TypeScript without seeing both standards.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 5. SUPPORTING ASSETS\n154: \n155: Component authoring (`assets/checklists/`): `skill-authoring.md`, `agent-authoring.md`, `command-authoring.md`, `mcp-server-authoring.md`\n156: \n157: Language quality gates (`assets/checklists/`): `universal-checklist.md`, `typescript-checklist.md`, `python-checklist.md`, `shell-checklist.md`, `javascript-checklist.md`, `rust-checklist/` (split into topic parts), `config-checklist.md`\n158: \n159: Verifier assets (`assets/scripts/`): alignment-drift and stack-folder verifier scripts, the router-sync guard (`verify-router-sync.cjs`) with its replay library (`router-replay-lib.cjs`) and the documentation claim guard (`verify-doc-claims.cjs`), used by this surface. `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` is the single entry point that runs all four as one gate (non-zero if any fails).\n160: \n161: Changelog directories (`changelog/`): real skill and packet changelog files are part of the Skilled surface inventory, not out-of-scope documentation noise.\n162: \n163: Daemon IPC/socket wiring: daemon-backed CLIs and plugin bridges resolve runtime Unix socket paths such as `daemon-ipc.sock`; sockets are runtime artifacts, so checked-in evidence lives in launcher, bridge, and config files rather than in committed `.sock` files.\n164: \n165: Spec-folder authoring lives in system-spec-kit (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` + `spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`), not in this surface. Checklists are pulled on demand by the active workflow phase.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 6. QUALITY CHECKLIST MAP\n170: \n171: This is this repository's quality checklist map, not a universal one.\n172: The quality runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows owned by the quality mode in `../sk-code-quality/assets/quality-defaults.json` and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration in `../mode-registry.json`.\n173: This table renders both sources in that order.\n174: `scripts/tests/quality-checklist-map.test.cjs` fails when the two differ.\n175: In any repository, run `node ../sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` to see the checklists for a file and why they apply.\n176: \n177: A SKILLED row applies only to a file that resolves to SKILLED.\n178: A `sk-code-ci` row applies by path alone because that surface declares no detection block.\n179: The spec-folder row applies only inside this hub's own repository.\n180: Applies to lists path globs and extensions. A row with several values matches on any of them.\n181: The MCP server checklist, `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`, has no path to declare, so load it by judgment when your target is MCP server source.\n182: \n183: | Owner | Applies to | Checklist |\n184: | --- | --- | --- |\n185: | quality defaults | `**` | `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` |\n186: | quality defaults | `specs/**` | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` |\n187: | SKILLED | `.skilled/**` | `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n188: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/**` | `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n189: | SKILLED | `.skilled/agents/**` | `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n190: | SKILLED | `.skilled/commands/**` | `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n191: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.cjs`, `.mjs` | `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n192: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md` |\n193: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md` |\n194: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md` |\n195: | SKILLED | `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.mts`, `.cts` | `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n196: | SKILLED | `.py` | `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n197: | SKILLED | `.sh`, `.bash` | `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n198: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md` |\n199: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md` |\n200: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md` |\n201: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md` |\n202: | SKILLED | `.json`, `.jsonc`, `.yaml`, `.yml` | `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n203: | sk-code-ci | `.github/workflows/**` | `../sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md, lines 1-203\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-skilled\n3: description: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.1.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8:   packetKind: surface\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n12: \n13: **Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n14: \n15: Detection has two steps:\n16: \n17: - First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n18: - Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n19:   - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n20:   - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n21:   - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n22:   - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n23:   - `.rs` -> Rust\n24:   - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n25: \n26: For Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\n27: napi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n28: \n29: ## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n30: \n31: - The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n32: - The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n33: - This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n38: \n39: The hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n40: \n41: The parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n42: \n43: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n48: \n49: Language standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n50: - TypeScript:\n51:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n52:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n53:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n54:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n55:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n56:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n57:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n58: - Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n59: - Shell:\n60:   - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n61:   - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n62:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n63:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n64:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n65:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n66: - Rust:\n67:   - Style guide:\n68:     - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n69:     - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n70:     - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n71:     - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n72:     - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n73:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n74:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n75:   - Quality standards:\n76:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n77:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n78:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n79:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n80:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n81:   - Quick reference:\n82:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n83:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n84:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n85:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n86:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n87: - Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n88: - JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n89: \n90: Language-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n91: - `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n92: - `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n93: - `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n94: - `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n95: \n96: Authoring and validation assets:\n97: \n98: - Checklists:\n99:   - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n100:   - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n101:   - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n102:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n103:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n104:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n105:   - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n106:   - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n107:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n108:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n109:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n110:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n111:   - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n112:   - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n113:   - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n114:   - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n115: - Scripts:\n116:   - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n117: - References:\n118:   - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n119:   - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n120: \n121: Workflow: `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md` link to the hub's shared implement -> debug -> verify doctrine in `../../shared/references/`; the split shared, language, hook and alignment resources above apply on top of it.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 4. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n126: \n127: - **Plugins never write to the TUI.** OpenCode plugins must not print to the process stdout/stderr (no overlay on the chat input); user/agent-visible output goes through system-context injection, tools, or append-only log files; DEBUG-gated stderr is allowed only behind an env flag. See `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md` and the plugin exemption tier.\n128: - **Descriptors are load-bearing.** `graph-metadata.json` / `description.json` shape drives discovery; validate JSON/JSONC against `references/config/quality-standards.md`.\n129: - **Alignment drift is a verification gate.** Before any completion claim, re-run the four live sk-code drift guards on a system-code change through the single entry point `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh`.\n130:   - `assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` checks language integrity, plus dead RESOURCE_MAP routes with `--check-router`.\n131:   - `assets/scripts/verify_stack_folders.py` checks that language reference folders resolve.\n132:   - `assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs` is the router-sync guard. It restores the four checks of the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite, which was deleted, and the wrapper runs every leg of it: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4.\n133:     - Check (1): router paths exist on disk, every routable doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed.\n134:     - Check (2): the parent RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier.\n135:     - Check (3): compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and this RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`.\n136:     - Check (4): every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router.\n137:   - The dead-path part of check (1) is also checked by `--check-router`. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc walk, fails on any reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink.\n138:   - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4) in CI only, with the admission step warn-only.\n139:   - The documentation claim guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) reads the sk-code prose the other guards skip, from the hub folder on disk.\n140:     It checks that path references resolve and that no retired packet name remains.\n141:     It also checks that every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json`, and that every file the `ROUTER.md` load-tier prose says loads on every route is in `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.\n142:     Lines that keep a legacy name on purpose sit in its allowlist with a reason. See `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`.\n143:   The wrapper scans repository content only: the files git tracks, so ignored trees, sibling worktrees and vendored clones stay out of a gate they can never pass. It exits 0 on a clean tree, so a completion claim requires wrapper rc 0 rather than a delta against a backlog.\n144: - **Rust preserves the TypeScript contract.** Rust napi-rs, WASM/WASI, and sidecar modules are compatibility implementations, not independent behavior authorities. JS-visible bytes, six-decimal numeric behavior, comparator tie-breaks, deterministic IDs, collection order, DTOs, and error shapes must remain identical to the TypeScript oracle.\n145: - **Touched-language set, not one-per-task.** Most `.skilled/` tasks touch a single language. Keep that slice tight and use the shared tier for cross-language rules.\n146: \n147: An interop task that spans a language pair, such as a napi-rs / WASM / sidecar Rust module held to its TypeScript oracle, legitimately touches both languages.\n148: The router selects the languages the task actually touches and loads both trios plus the shared tier.\n149: You cannot hold Rust byte-identical to TypeScript without seeing both standards.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 5. SUPPORTING ASSETS\n154: \n155: Component authoring (`assets/checklists/`): `skill-authoring.md`, `agent-authoring.md`, `command-authoring.md`, `mcp-server-authoring.md`\n156: \n157: Language quality gates (`assets/checklists/`): `universal-checklist.md`, `typescript-checklist.md`, `python-checklist.md`, `shell-checklist.md`, `javascript-checklist.md`, `rust-checklist/` (split into topic parts), `config-checklist.md`\n158: \n159: Verifier assets (`assets/scripts/`): alignment-drift and stack-folder verifier scripts, the router-sync guard (`verify-router-sync.cjs`) with its replay library (`router-replay-lib.cjs`) and the documentation claim guard (`verify-doc-claims.cjs`), used by this surface. `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` is the single entry point that runs all four as one gate (non-zero if any fails).\n160: \n161: Changelog directories (`changelog/`): real skill and packet changelog files are part of the Skilled surface inventory, not out-of-scope documentation noise.\n162: \n163: Daemon IPC/socket wiring: daemon-backed CLIs and plugin bridges resolve runtime Unix socket paths such as `daemon-ipc.sock`; sockets are runtime artifacts, so checked-in evidence lives in launcher, bridge, and config files rather than in committed `.sock` files.\n164: \n165: Spec-folder authoring lives in system-spec-kit (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` + `spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`), not in this surface. Checklists are pulled on demand by the active workflow phase.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 6. QUALITY CHECKLIST MAP\n170: \n171: This is this repository's quality checklist map, not a universal one.\n172: The quality runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows owned by the quality mode in `../sk-code-quality/assets/quality-defaults.json` and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration in `../mode-registry.json`.\n173: This table renders both sources in that order.\n174: `scripts/tests/quality-checklist-map.test.cjs` fails when the two differ.\n175: In any repository, run `node ../sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` to see the checklists for a file and why they apply.\n176: \n177: A SKILLED row applies only to a file that resolves to SKILLED.\n178: A `sk-code-ci` row applies by path alone because that surface declares no detection block.\n179: The spec-folder row applies only inside this hub's own repository.\n180: Applies to lists path globs and extensions. A row with several values matches on any of them.\n181: The MCP server checklist, `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`, has no path to declare, so load it by judgment when your target is MCP server source.\n182: \n183: | Owner | Applies to | Checklist |\n184: | --- | --- | --- |\n185: | quality defaults | `**` | `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` |\n186: | quality defaul